T25.01|Market risk comes from changing prices and rates; credit risk from failure to fulfil obligations; liquidity risk from inability to obtain cash or transact affordably; operational risk from failed processes, people, systems or external events. These categories interact rather than forming independent boxes.~~A market loss can trigger margin calls, force asset sales and expose operational weaknesses. Analyse the chain of cash demands as well as the initial accounting loss.|Market: Adverse price or rate movement.;;Credit: Default or deterioration in repayment.;;Liquidity/operational: Funding and execution failures.|A firm has ₹10 lakh cash and loses ₹8 on marked-to-market positions, with a ₹12 margin call due immediately.~~Recognise the market loss.;;Compare the call with available cash: ₹2 shortfall.;;Assess financing and sale capacity before settlement.~~A solvent-looking firm can face an urgent liquidity failure.~~Accounting value does not establish cash accessibility.|Map risk interactions.;;Distinguish losses from cash calls.;;Test time-critical funding.
T25.02|Business risk arises from operating economics; strategic risk from choices about markets, products and capabilities; reputational risk from lost trust. They can change revenue, cost, access to finance and the company's long-run competitive position. Financial hedges generally cannot neutralise a flawed business model.~~Risk assessment should identify leading evidence, decision owners and responses, rather than assigning a score with no operating consequence.|Business risk: Demand, margins and execution uncertainty.;;Strategic risk: Wrong direction or resource allocation.;;Reputational risk: Loss of stakeholder confidence.|A company with ₹100 lakh sales and 20% contribution margin suffers a 15% sales loss after a product-quality incident.~~Contribution falls by ₹3 if variable economics remain unchanged.;;Add recall and remediation costs separately.;;Assess whether trust and demand recover or remain impaired.~~An initial incident can damage recurring economics.~~Fixed costs and financing may amplify the earnings effect.|Connect risks to cash drivers.;;Assign accountable owners.;;Monitor recovery evidence and second-order effects.
T25.03|Counterparty risk is exposure to another party failing to perform. Settlement risk is the possibility that one side delivers cash or assets without receiving the agreed exchange. Replacement cost, future exposure, collateral and close-out rights determine the economic loss.~~Delivery-versus-payment or payment-versus-payment can reduce principal settlement risk where available, but do not eliminate every credit, liquidity or operational risk.|Pre-settlement exposure: Potential loss before exchange completion.;;Replacement cost: Cost to replace a favourable contract.;;Settlement principal risk: Delivered amount not received in return.|A derivative has ₹8 lakh positive value and ₹5 eligible collateral when the counterparty defaults.~~Uncollateralised exposure is ₹3.;;At 40% recovery, loss on that exposure is ₹1.8.;;Review close-out timing and collateral availability.~~Notional amount is not always current credit exposure.~~Legal netting, wrong-way risk and future price moves affect the result.|Verify enforceable netting.;;Monitor collateral gaps.;;Map settlement timing and principal risk.
T25.04|Concentration means dependence on a narrow set of issuers, industries, counterparties or common drivers. Contagion spreads stress through funding, ownership, payment networks or behaviour. Many individually small positions can share one underlying vulnerability.~~Look through legal entities to economic dependence. Suppliers, customers and banks can all fail under the same regional or commodity shock.|Name concentration: Exposure to one borrower or issuer.;;Factor concentration: Shared industry, geography or driver.;;Network contagion: Stress transmitted through connected entities.|Five borrowers each represent 10% of a loan book, all dependent on one commodity.~~Combined commodity-linked exposure is 50%.;;A 20% loss across those loans costs 10% of the whole book.;;Assess correlated defaults instead of assuming independent failures.~~Diversified names can conceal concentrated economics.~~Recovery and timing also become correlated in stress.|Aggregate shared drivers.;;Inspect counterparty networks.;;Stress common shocks and forced sales.
T25.05|Value at risk is a loss quantile for a specified horizon and confidence under a model. A 99% one-day VaR is not a maximum possible loss: the model allows worse outcomes in its remaining tail. Expected shortfall describes average loss in the worst specified fraction, giving more information about tail severity.~~Both depend on positions, data and modelling assumptions. Report confidence, horizon, currency and method; a bare number is incomplete.|VaR: Threshold at a selected loss percentile.;;Expected shortfall: Average loss across the selected worst tail.;;Stress loss: Loss under a defined severe scenario.|A model estimates one-day 99% VaR ₹5 lakh and 99% ES ₹8.~~The VaR is the 99th-percentile loss threshold.;;The worst 1% average ₹8 in this model.;;A ₹12 loss is not ruled out.~~Tail severity exceeds the VaR threshold.~~Real outcomes may differ because the model or dependence assumptions fail.|State horizon and confidence.;;Inspect tail and stress losses.;;Backtest and investigate exceptions.
T25.06|Historical risk simulation replays observed market changes; parametric methods use an assumed distribution and exposure model; Monte Carlo generates scenarios from specified stochastic processes. Each trades transparency, computational effort and model assumptions differently.~~More simulated paths reduce sampling noise but cannot repair a wrong process or missing risk factor. Nonlinear instruments require valuation that reflects changing sensitivities.|Historical: Applies past shocks to today's portfolio.;;Parametric: Uses distribution and sensitivity assumptions.;;Monte Carlo: Samples from a designed model.|A linear portfolio has ₹100 lakh exposure and estimated daily volatility 1%; assume zero mean and a normal model.~~Daily value volatility is ₹1 lakh.;;Approximate 99% VaR is 2.33 × ₹1 = ₹2.33.;;Compare with historical stress and nonlinear repricing.~~The estimate follows the assumptions rather than proving them.~~Fat tails, changing volatility and option convexity can invalidate it.|Choose a model suited to exposure.;;Document dependence assumptions.;;Compare alternative estimates and stress tests.
T25.07|Stress testing estimates outcomes under severe conditions. Reverse stress testing starts with a failure threshold and asks what combinations of events could produce it. Neither automatically assigns a reliable probability to the scenario.~~The purpose is to expose fragile dependencies and actionable mitigations. Include cash access, collateral calls and operational capacity alongside reported capital.|Sensitivity: Changes one driver.;;Scenario stress: Changes related drivers together.;;Reverse stress: Works backward from failure.|A business has ₹20 lakh equity; each 1% sales decline reduces equity by ₹1 through losses in a simplified horizon.~~A 20% decline exhausts equity under that relationship.;;Add interest and collection delays to test other failure paths.;;Identify actions before the threshold is reached.~~Failure may result from a combination milder than one isolated shock.~~Real relationships can become nonlinear under stress.|Define the failure condition.;;Test interacting shocks.;;Link results to contingency actions.
T25.08|Scenario design constructs coherent economic stories and translates them into operational and financial inputs. A recession might affect sales, margins, defaults, rates and liquidity together, rather than giving every variable an unrelated extreme.~~Separate severity, plausibility and probability. Scenarios support decisions even when probabilities are uncertain, but should not be presented as forecasts merely because they have detailed numbers.|Macro scenario: Economy-wide linked shocks.;;Idiosyncratic scenario: Company-specific disruption.;;Combined scenario: External shock plus internal vulnerability.|A case assumes sales fall from ₹100 to ₹85 lakh, contribution margin from 30% to 25%, and fixed cost stays ₹20.~~Initial contribution is ₹30 and operating profit ₹10.;;Stressed contribution is ₹21.25.;;Stressed operating profit is ₹1.25 before financing.~~Volume and margin interact to reduce profit sharply.~~Working capital and debt service need separate modelling.|Write the causal story.;;Keep assumptions internally consistent.;;Include timing and management responses.
T25.09|Risk appetite expresses the amount and kinds of uncertainty an organisation accepts while pursuing goals. Limits translate that policy into measurable boundaries; governance specifies who monitors, challenges and approves exceptions. A limit without reliable data and escalation has little force.~~Limits should reflect business scale, concentration, liquidity and capital. Crossing one should trigger a defined response rather than automatic acceptance because a position is profitable.|Appetite: Board-level risk preference and capacity.;;Limit: Operational measurable boundary.;;Escalation: Required review and corrective response.|A policy caps one-counterparty exposure at 10% of ₹200 lakh capital.~~Limit is ₹20.;;A new trade raising exposure from ₹18 to ₹23 breaches by ₹3.;;Require authorised reduction or documented exception before proceeding.~~Profitable expected returns do not override the rule.~~Exposure definitions and approval rights must be specified.|Define measurable limits.;;Assign independent oversight.;;Document breaches, exceptions and remediation.
T25.10|Credit portfolio risk depends on default probability, exposure, loss severity and how borrowers fail together. Expected loss is a probability-weighted average; unexpected loss concerns adverse variation beyond that average. Sector correlation can make portfolio tails much worse than an independent-default model suggests.~~Loan pricing, provisions and capital serve different purposes. No single expected-loss estimate captures liquidity, concentration and model uncertainty.|PD: Probability of default over a horizon.;;LGD: Fraction lost after recovery and costs.;;EAD: Exposure when default occurs.|For ₹100 lakh EAD, 2% PD and 40% LGD, use a simple one-period model.~~Expected loss = 100 × 0.02 × 0.40 = ₹0.8.;;A realised default may lose ₹40.;;Stress correlated defaults across other borrowers.~~A small average loss can coexist with a severe realised loss.~~Undrawn commitments and recovery timing change EAD and LGD.|Estimate consistent horizons.;;Model correlated defaults.;;Distinguish provisions, pricing and capital.
T25.11|Asset-liability mismatch occurs when assets and obligations differ in timing, repricing, currency or liquidity. A positive net worth does not guarantee that payments can be made when due. Long-lived assets financed with short-term borrowing expose the borrower to rollover risk.~~Match the economic sensitivity as well as calendar maturity. A floating-rate asset and fixed-rate liability can react differently even with the same final date.|Maturity mismatch: Different payment dates.;;Repricing mismatch: Rates reset differently.;;Currency mismatch: Cash earned and owed in different currencies.|A company has ₹100 lakh five-year assets and ₹80 debt due next month, but only ₹10 liquid cash.~~Immediate refinancing need is ₹70 if assets cannot be sold.;;A 20% asset write-down leaves assets ₹80 before considering cash classification.;;Assess both solvency and funding timing separately.~~Long-term asset value cannot settle a near-term bill by itself.~~Avoid double-counting cash inside total assets.|Map dated cash flows.;;Stress refinancing availability.;;Measure rate and currency gaps.
T25.12|Model risk is loss from incorrect models, inputs, implementation or use. Validation challenges conceptual soundness, data, calculations and suitability for the decision. A technically accurate calculation can still be inappropriate for a changed business environment.~~Independent review should examine limitations, overrides and monitoring. Good historical fit is weak evidence when the model uses future information or has been repeatedly tuned to the same sample.|Conceptual error: Wrong economic representation.;;Implementation error: Code or formula differs from design.;;Use error: Application outside intended conditions.|A credit model trained on benign years predicts 1% defaults; a downturn produces 5%.~~Check population and economic changes.;;Separate data defects from model assumptions.;;Recalibrate or restrict use with independent review.~~Unexpected outcomes demand diagnosis, not automatic parameter adjustment.~~One period alone may be noisy, so inspect wider evidence.|Validate independently.;;Monitor performance and drift.;;Record limits, overrides and ownership.
T25.13|Operational, cyber and fraud risks arise from processes and human or system failures, including unauthorised actions. Controls can prevent incidents, detect them and reduce their consequences. Recovery planning is essential because prevention is never perfect.~~Access separation, reconciliation and tested backups address different failure paths. Counting policies is less informative than checking whether controls work in realistic conditions.|Preventive: Stops an unauthorised or erroneous action.;;Detective: Reveals an incident or discrepancy.;;Corrective: Restores service and limits harm.|A payment clerk can create suppliers and approve transfers; an unauthorised ₹5 lakh payment occurs.~~Separate creation from approval authority.;;Reconcile bank movements independently.;;Test recovery and incident escalation.~~A concentration of permissions enabled the loss.~~Controls should balance timely operations with independent verification.|Map critical processes.;;Test access and reconciliation.;;Rehearse recovery and escalation.
T25.14|Enterprise risk management examines risks across the whole organisation and relates them to strategy, capital and resilience. It avoids treating departments as isolated portfolios when they share customers, technology or funding. Risk aggregation requires judgment because categories can overlap.~~ERM should influence resource allocation and operating decisions, not merely compile a register. Accountability and a usable escalation process make the framework practical.|Identification: Maps risks and dependencies.;;Assessment: Evaluates severity, timing and interaction.;;Response: Avoids, reduces, transfers or accepts exposure.|Sales depends on one customer and operations on one cloud provider; both are vulnerable to the same regional outage.~~Map their common dependency.;;Estimate lost revenue and service interruption together.;;Assign a contingency owner and test alternatives.~~Separate department scores can understate enterprise vulnerability.~~Avoid double-counting losses when aggregating overlapping effects.|Connect risks to strategy.;;Assess common dependencies.;;Track mitigations and residual exposure.
T25.15|Economic capital estimates resources needed to absorb unexpected losses under an internal risk standard. Risk-adjusted performance compares earnings with the capital required to support them. The answer depends on horizon, confidence, loss definition and allocation method.~~A high accounting return may simply use more hidden tail risk. Regulatory capital and economic capital can differ because they serve different frameworks.|Economic capital: Internally estimated risk-bearing resources.;;RAROC: Risk-adjusted earnings relative to allocated capital.;;Hurdle comparison: Tests return against required compensation.|A unit earns ₹8 lakh before expected credit loss ₹2 and uses ₹40 economic capital; ignore taxes and other adjustments.~~Risk-adjusted profit is ₹6.;;Illustrative RAROC is 6 ÷ 40 = 15%.;;Compare with the stated hurdle and other risks.~~Risk adjustment changes the apparent business return.~~Definitions of income and capital must be consistent.|Specify the risk standard.;;Avoid double-counting expected losses.;;Test capital allocation sensitivity.
T25.16|Systemic risk threatens the functioning of the financial system through interconnected exposures, funding runs, fire sales or critical-service disruption. A system can be fragile even when individual firms appear compliant, because their responses to stress reinforce each other.~~Crises often combine leverage, maturity mismatch and confidence loss. Policy interventions can contain damage but also change incentives and redistribute costs.|Contagion: Losses transmit through connections.;;Common exposure: Many firms face the same shock.;;Feedback loop: Defensive actions worsen system conditions.|Several funds hold the same asset financed short term; falling prices trigger margin calls.~~Funds sell to raise cash.;;Sales depress prices and trigger further calls.;;Assess funding resilience and crowded ownership.~~Individually rational selling can amplify aggregate loss.~~The example illustrates a mechanism, not a prediction of a particular crisis.|Map interconnections.;;Stress simultaneous responses.;;Study leverage and funding feedbacks.
